---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DraftHeader from '../../components/draft/DraftHeader.astro';
import DraftContact from '../../components/draft/DraftContact.astro';
import DraftCaseNav from '../../components/draft/DraftCaseNav.astro';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';
import '../../styles/draft.css';

import designSystem1 from '../../assets/images/design-system-1.png';
import designSystem2 from '../../assets/images/design-system-2.png';
import appDsAlerts from '../../assets/images/raa-app-ds-alerts.png';
import dsButtons from '../../assets/images/raa-ds-buttons.png';
import uofaStyleguide from '../../assets/images/uofa-styleguide.png';
---
<Layout title="Design Systems | Andrew Smith" description="Design systems built so product and engineering teams can move faster without design as a bottleneck on standard patterns." showFooterCta={false}>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <DraftHeader />
    <main id="main-content">

      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>Design Systems</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Design systems for consistency at scale.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">When components are documented and governed properly, product and engineering teams can move faster without design as a bottleneck on standard patterns. Designers write lighter specs, testing increases, and the time that is freed up goes toward the discovery and validation work that actually moves product quality forward.</p>
        <p class="hero-subtext">At RAA, a web design system and a native app design system, built in-house by the team. At the University of Adelaide, a custom JavaScript design system.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Web platform, native app, University of Adelaide</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead, UX Manager</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Figma, ZeroHeight, design tokens</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={designSystem2} alt="Component documentation showing anatomy, usage guidelines, and design specifications" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">The Approach</p>
        <h2>Governed for consistency, documented for adoption.</h2>
        <div class="summary-body">
          <p>The RAA design system was built in-house by the team, in Figma, as a component library with design tokens, and has grown over the years. More recently we have been moving it onto a design system platform, and I led that work with the team. The decisions made when a system is first established — about naming, structure, token architecture, and documentation — shape how the system behaves for years. A system governed with engineering adoption in mind gets used. One that is left to drift gets worked around.</p>
          <p>Components are documented with anatomy, spacing, typography, states, accessibility considerations, dos and don'ts, and coded examples. Governance means the system is maintained over time — new components are added deliberately, old ones are deprecated cleanly, and teams know where to look for the source of truth.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Web Design System</p>
          <h2>Governing the full RAA web platform.</h2>
          <p>The RAA web design system covers all consumer-facing digital products — insurance, membership, travel, payments, identity management, and the public website. Built in Figma with a component library, design tokens, and documentation published to ZeroHeight for engineering adoption.</p>
          <p>Accessibility standards are built into the system at the component level through the Vision Australia partnership — WCAG compliance is designed in from the start, not audited after delivery. Components include accessibility annotations alongside visual specs so engineers have what they need without a separate handover conversation.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="full-image-section">
        <Image class="full-image" src={designSystem1} alt="Design system component library showing button variants, form elements, and navigation patterns" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">App Design System</p>
          <h2>Native iOS and Android component governance.</h2>
          <p>The RAA app design system covers the native iOS and Android application. Alert components, button variants, navigation patterns, and mobile-specific interactions governed through a documented Figma library with light and dark mode variants for every component.</p>
          <p>The system was built to align with platform conventions — Material Design for Android, Apple Human Interface Guidelines for iOS — while maintaining RAA brand consistency. Components are built for engineering handover with variant properties, interaction states, and platform-specific guidance included.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="full-image-section">
        <Image class="full-image" src={appDsAlerts} alt="RAA app design system showing alert components in light and dark mode" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="full-image-section">
        <Image class="full-image" src={dsButtons} alt="Button component documentation showing anatomy, spacing, typography, states, accessibility guidelines, and coded examples" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">University of Adelaide</p>
          <h2>A JavaScript design system that grew from the website build.</h2>
          <p>I was on the University of Adelaide project from the start. I wrote the code for the website build. That code became part of a custom JavaScript design system. Each component is documented in a style guide with code examples.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="full-image-section">
        <figure>
          <Image class="full-image" src={uofaStyleguide} alt="University of Adelaide style guide page documenting the audio player and playlist components, with HTML code examples" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
          <figcaption>University of Adelaide style guide</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <DraftCaseNav />
    </main>
    <DraftContact />
  </div>
</Layout>
